Q: Suddenly application starts throwing 500 errors. What to do ? provide steps.
    When an application suddenly starts throwing 500 Internal Server Errors in production, a systematic triage approach is required
    to isolate whether the issue is infrastructural, code-related, or caused by a downstream dependency.
        1.Check Centralized Logs & Dashboards:
            Step 1: Triage
            Navigate to your log aggregation tool (ELK, Datadog, CloudWatch) and APM (New Relic, Dynatrace) to isolate the
            exact stack trace, exception type, and the affected API endpoint.
            Verify success by identifying the root exception class (e.g., NullPointerException, DataAccessException, or TimeoutException)
            and the timestamp when the spike began.
        2.Inspect Container & Pod Health:
            Step 2: Infrastructure
            Check Kubernetes or AWS ECS resource metrics to see if pods are restarting (OOMKilled), throttling CPU,
            or failing liveness/readiness probes.Verify success by checking pod restart counts using kubectl get pods and
            ensuring CPU/Memory usage is well below limits.
        3.Verify Downstream Dependencies:
            Step 3: Dependencies.
            Check the health of external integrations, databases, caches (Redis), and message brokers (Kafka) that the service relies on.
            Verify success by confirming dependency health endpoints return 200 OK and connection pools are active.
        4.Review Recent Deployments and Configs:
            Step 4: Change Management
            Check CI/CD pipelines, Git commit history, or recent environment variable/feature flag updates to see if a faulty deployment
            or bad config change was pushed. Verify success by correlating the error spike start time with the exact deployment timestamp.
        5.Execute Rollback or Hotfix:
            Step 5: Mitigation
            If a recent deployment caused the issue, immediately trigger a rollback via your deployment pipeline, or apply a hotfix
            if the bug is isolated. Verify success by monitoring the error rate drop back to baseline levels on the APM dashboard.
