Q: Microservices not responding within time - what to investigate and and resolve ?
    When microservices fail to respond within an acceptable timeframe, it typically points to resource starvation, network issues, or cascading blocks.
    1. Determine the scope
        Check:
            Which API?
            Which service?
            All requests or specific requests?
            All instances or only some instances?
            Since when?
            Is traffic unusually high?

    2. Trace the request
        Use distributed tracing:
            Trace ID: abc123
                API Gateway       50 ms
                Order Service    100 ms
                Payment Service  900 ms
                DB               700 ms
            Now I know where to investigate.

    3. Check thread pools
        A service may have sufficient CPU but still be slow because worker threads are blocked.
        Check:
            Tomcat/Jetty thread pool
            Kafka consumer threads
            blocked threads
            thread dumps

    4. Check downstream services
        Investigate:
            REST dependency latency
            DB latency
            Kafka delays
            external APIs
            Redis/cache
            network issues

    7. Check JVM
        Look for:
            high CPU
            excessive GC
            memory leaks
            thread contention

    8. Check infrastructure
        Check:
            pod restarts
            CPU throttling
            memory limits
            node issues
